  "clipRounding": {
    "description": "A magazine holds whole rounds, and a weapon that fires in bursts holds whole bursts, so a computed clip is rounded up to a multiple of the burst size. That is EDSY's rule (`ceil(ammoclip / bstsize) * bstsize`, applied where the blueprint roll is stored), and it reduces to Coriolis's plain round-up (`Module.getClip`, \"Clip size is always rounded up\") wherever a weapon fires one round at a time. It belongs where the roll is computed rather than where a stat is read, because a journal states the engineered clip itself and that figure is the game's own. A computed reserve rounds to the nearest whole round after all contributions are applied, matching Frontier's stated ammunition values.",
    "cases": [
      {
        "note": "A weapon that fires one round at a time: the burst step is inert and the clip simply rounds up.",
        "symbol": "Hpt_Cannon_Fixed_Small",
        "blueprint": "Weapon_HighCapacity",
        "grade": 3,
        "quality": 1,
        "baseAmmoClipSize": 6,
        "unroundedAmmoClipSize": 10.08,
        "burstSize": 1,
        "burstFrom": "none",
        "AmmoClipSize": 11,
        "AmmoMaximum": 202
      },
      {
        "note": "Double Shot scales the clip and gives the weapon a two-round burst in the same roll, so 4.02 rounds to a whole burst — 6, where a bare round-up would say 5. The burst is the recipe's own.",
        "symbol": "Hpt_Slugshot_Fixed_Medium",
        "blueprint": "Weapon_DoubleShot",
        "grade": 1,
        "quality": 1,
        "baseAmmoClipSize": 3,
        "unroundedAmmoClipSize": 4.02,
        "burstSize": 2,
        "burstFrom": "recipe",
        "AmmoClipSize": 6
      },
      {
        "note": "A Concord Cannon already fires three-round bursts and no recipe names them, so the burst comes from the weapon's own record: High Capacity's 12.24 becomes 15, where a bare round-up would say 13. This is the case that separates EDSY's rule from Coriolis's.",
        "symbol": "Hpt_Cannon_Gimbal_Medium_Burst",
        "blueprint": "Weapon_HighCapacity",
        "grade": 1,
        "quality": 1,
        "baseAmmoClipSize": 9,
        "unroundedAmmoClipSize": 12.24,
        "burstSize": 3,
        "burstFrom": "module",
        "AmmoClipSize": 15,
        "AmmoMaximum": 408
      },
      {
        "note": "The same fragment cannon under High Capacity: the clip rounds up and the reserve rounds to the nearest whole round.",
        "symbol": "Hpt_Slugshot_Fixed_Medium",
        "blueprint": "Weapon_HighCapacity",
        "grade": 3,
        "quality": 1,
        "baseAmmoClipSize": 3,
        "unroundedAmmoClipSize": 5.04,
        "burstSize": 1,
        "burstFrom": "none",
        "AmmoClipSize": 6,
        "AmmoMaximum": 302
      },
      {
        "note": "The registries state their multipliers to three decimals, so Drag Munitions' +66.7% computes 10.002 on a 6-round rack where the recipe means 10. A figure within that precision of a whole magazine is snapped to it before the round-up, or the transcription noise buys a whole extra round. The reserve's 30.006 rounds normally to 30.",
        "symbol": "Hpt_BasicMissileRack_Fixed_Medium",
        "blueprint": "SeekerMissileRack_Drag",
        "grade": 2,
        "quality": 1,
        "baseAmmoClipSize": 6,
        "unroundedAmmoClipSize": 10.002,
        "burstSize": 1,
        "burstFrom": "none",
        "AmmoClipSize": 10,
        "AmmoMaximum": 30
      },
      {
        "note": "A quality roll between two published legs is a real number with no whole magazine behind it, so nothing is snapped and the round-up stands: 185.12 rounds load as 186. This is the case that separates recovering a published figure from swallowing a fraction the recipe means — the snap applies to a stated multiplier only.",
        "symbol": "Hpt_MultiCannon_Fixed_Small",
        "blueprint": "Weapon_HighCapacity",
        "grade": 5,
        "quality": 0.07,
        "baseAmmoClipSize": 100,
        "unroundedAmmoClipSize": 185.12,
        "burstSize": 1,
        "burstFrom": "none",
        "AmmoClipSize": 186
      },
      {
        "note": "The round-up runs in one direction, not in the recipe's: a multi-cannon's Overcharged cuts the clip, and 76.5 rounds to 77 rather than to 76.",
        "symbol": "Hpt_MultiCannon_Gimbal_Medium",
        "blueprint": "MC_Overcharged",
        "grade": 5,
        "quality": 1,
        "baseAmmoClipSize": 90,
        "unroundedAmmoClipSize": 76.5,
        "burstSize": 1,
        "burstFrom": "none",
        "AmmoClipSize": 77
      },
      {
        "note": "A roll that moves the clip nowhere leaves it alone, even when the stock figure is not a whole number of bursts. High Capacity's grade-1 leg starts at +0%, and this accelerator's 18 rounds are 4.5 of its 4-round bursts; they stay 18. Reachable through `computeModifiers` only — no engineering menu offers this weapon anything, so `applyBlueprint` refuses it.",
        "symbol": "Hpt_MkIIPlasmaShockAutocannon_Fixed_Large",
        "blueprint": "Weapon_HighCapacity",
        "grade": 1,
        "quality": 0,
        "baseAmmoClipSize": 18,
        "unroundedAmmoClipSize": 18,
        "burstSize": 4,
        "burstFrom": "module",
        "AmmoClipSize": 18
      }
    ]
  },
  "journalSpellings": {
    "description": "Journal `EngineerModifications` spellings read off a real `StoredModules` capture (521 stored modules, contributed by the repository owner 2026-08-07 UTC; scrubbed of the commander, carrier and market it came from, which say nothing about the game). Of the 46 distinct spellings in that capture, 43 resolve against `BLUEPRINTS` and the other three are the `Decorative_*` liveries, which name no recipe at all and are pinned in \u00a7decorativeModifications. These are the cases worth pinning: an id whose key is not what the game writes, and one whose journal spelling the catalogue had missed entirely. `resolved` is what `resolveBlueprintForModule` answers \u2014 the `BLUEPRINTS` key the fitted module actually rolls, returned in the caller\u2019s own casing, since every lookup downstream matches case-insensitively.",
    "cases": [
      {
        "symbol": "Hpt_Guardian_GaussCannon_Fixed_Medium",
        "blueprint": "GuardianModule_Sturdy",
        "resolved": "GuardianModule_Sturdy",
        "note": "The game writes the module spelling on a Guardian *weapon*, so there is no second recipe for the weapon half and no second key for it."
      },
      {
        "symbol": "Hpt_MultiCannon_Gimbal_Huge",
        "blueprint": "Weapon_Overcharged",
        "resolved": "MC_Overcharged"
      },
      {
        "symbol": "Hpt_CrimeScanner_Size0_Class5",
        "blueprint": "Sensor_WideAngle",
        "resolved": "Scanner_WideAngle"
      },
      {
        "symbol": "Hpt_Guardian_ShardCannon_Fixed_Medium",
        "blueprint": "weapon_longrange",
        "resolved": "weapon_longrange",
        "note": "The game writes this one lower-case. Every lookup matches case-insensitively, so it resolves to `Weapon_LongRange` without being rewritten."
      }
    ],
    "operationsKeys": {
      "description": "Operations blueprints are keyed as the registry publishes them minus Inara\u2019s `recipe_` prefix \u2014 neither coriolis (81 keys, none prefixed) nor EDSY (no `recipe_` string at all) uses it, and no observed build does either. `prefixed` is empty and pins that there is no exception: the two Inara spellings of Anti-Guardian Zone Resistance are not stored, because that recipe's real name, `GuardianModule_Sturdy`, is a key in its own right and is what the game writes on Guardian weapons as well as modules. `observed` records a spelling read off a real Inara SLEF export contributed by the repository owner \u2014 a Mercenary Module Reinforcement Package taken to grade 5. The export writes it `modulereinforcement_heavyduty`; `blueprint` below is the catalogue key that id resolves to, whose casing comes from a raw journal rather than from Inara, which lower-cases everything it exports. Every lookup matches case-insensitively, so both forms reach the same record.",
      "prefixed": [],
      "observed": [
        {
          "symbol": "Int_ModuleReinforcement_Size5_Class2",
          "blueprint": "ModuleReinforcement_HeavyDuty",
          "grade": 5,
          "soldAtGrade": 1
        }
      ]
    }
  },
  "decorativeModifications": {
    "description": "The festive transformations the game writes in the same journal field as an engineering blueprint, read off the same `StoredModules` capture as §journalSpellings — it holds three medium turreted Remote Release Flak Launchers, one per colour. They are not engineering: no grade, no materials, and no engineer offers one. So they are absent from `BLUEPRINTS` and from every menu in `engineering-options`, and `DECORATIVE_MODIFICATIONS` is what makes the id resolve to something rather than to nothing — the distinction between \"this id is real and names no recipe\" and \"this id is unknown\", which is the whole reason the catalogue exists. They are not cosmetic-only either: a festive launcher fires fireworks rather than flak, and every one of them carries a single `Damage` modifier of -99%, so no record here may be read as \"this module is unmodified\". `module` is the only module observed carrying one, and the catalogue groups it nowhere: a Remote Release Flak Launcher takes no engineering, and being awarded one already transformed does not make it engineerable. `name` pairs the festive naming with the id's colour; no registry publishes the outfitting panel's own string.",
    "module": "Hpt_FlakMortar_Turret_Medium",
    "ids": [
      { "id": "Decorative_Green", "name": "Festive Green" },
      { "id": "Decorative_Red", "name": "Festive Red" },
      { "id": "Decorative_Yellow", "name": "Festive Yellow" }
    ],
    "modifiers": [{ "label": "Damage", "method": "multiplicative", "value": -0.99 }],
    "resolved": {
      "description": "What the stored modifier makes of the medium turreted Remote Release Flak Launcher, and the outfitting-panel reading it was derived from. The panel is rounded to one decimal, so it does not pin the modifier on its own — but the three figures together do: -99.0% of the 34 base damage is 0.34, which displays as 0.3, and 0.34 x the 0.5 rate of fire is 0.17, which displays as 0.2/s. An overwrite to a flat 0.3 would read -99.1% and 0.1/s, so the modifier is multiplicative and the panel's 0.3 is a rounding. Recomputing these is what would catch the modifier being re-entered as the displayed value.",
      "baseDamage": 34,
      "damage": 0.34,
      "damagePerSecond": 0.17,
      "panel": { "percent": -99, "damage": 0.3, "damagePerSecond": 0.2 }
    }
  },
  "journalNames": {
    "description": "Every `BLUEPRINTS` record whose key is NOT the `BlueprintName` the game writes, mapped to the id it is written as. Three of the 107; of the rest, 79 are already the journal id and 25 are Operations keys for which no journal spelling has been observed, which is why the field is absent from them. Each of the three shares its journal id with another record that rolls different numbers, so reading the id alone would fold the wrong recipe: the two scanner ids are §scannerIdCollision, and `MC_Overcharged` is the multi-cannon Overcharged, which cuts the clip by 3-15% where the `Weapon_Overcharged` every other weapon menu lists leaves it alone. `resolveBlueprintForModule` settles each by asking which recipe the fitted module's own menu offers.",
    "map": {
      "MC_Overcharged": "Weapon_Overcharged",
      "Scanner_LongRange": "Sensor_LongRange",
      "Scanner_WideAngle": "Sensor_WideAngle"
    }
  },
  "reachability": {
